ev('EVD122',"Direct check of DNA 6035F (SRC067; file 0407565, the same document number as Hastings's DOE URL '7565_.pdf'): (a) The entry is real and complete in a 538-page copy that is publicly available now: Table 60 'USS Curtiss (AV-4) operational activities during the CASTLE test series' (printed p.340-341; source line 'USS Curtiss (AV-4) Deck log'), KOON (B) 7 Apr, det. 0620, post-shot column: 'at 1138 anchored berth N-6, Bikini; at 1948 left berth en route to Enewetak; at 2305 an unidentified luminous object passed over ship from bow to stern, yellowish-orange in color, traveling at a high rate of speed and a low altitude'. Verified (CLM218). (b) The book's quotation is a composite: its opening clause 'at 0408 on station in operating area BH 35-40-L' belongs to the UNION row (26 April), not 7 April; so the quoted text is not a verbatim single log entry (CLM219). (c) The table is DNA's summary of the deck log, not a typed transcription, which weakens Hastings's argument that Handelman's disputed wording proves later alteration, and fits Handelman's doubt that he wrote those words. (d) The 'missing pages 301-350' claim is not supported: the full document, including Table 60, is available at the mirror, and the DTRA copy is listed in search results; I cannot check the DOE OHP site's history, so whether pages were once absent there is unknown, but the sighting is accessible (CLM222 disputed). (e) The claim that the captain had the entry struck is contradicted by the entry's survival (the book itself notes this) (CLM221). (f) The Koon shot was at 0620, so the 2305 sighting was ~16.7 h later, not 'just hours' after. (g) Aldrich's catalogue of seagoing reports (Project 1947 snippet) lists the same entry, sourcing it to the Curtiss log book.",'GOVERNMENT_RECORD','SRC067','INC098;CLM218;CLM219;CLM221;CLM222','4071','HIGH','VERIFIED')
ev('EVD123',"Sky at the Curtiss sighting (analysis/curtiss_1954_sky.py; time zone assumed UTC+12 for the log): at 2305 local the Sun was -56 deg, the 22% waning Moon was at the western horizon (alt -2, az 296), Jupiter (m-1.9) alt 8 W-WNW, Saturn alt 36 ESE, Mars below the horizon; no bright body was overhead. A fast yellowish-orange object passing overhead bow to stern is therefore not a star or planet; a meteor/fireball, aircraft (Handelman's 'foreign spy plane'), or ship's own lights are the unexamined candidates. Zigzags are hearsay.",'ANALYST_CALCULATION','analysis/curtiss_1954_sky.py','INC098;CLM220','4071','LOW','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
